INT. FUNICULAR CAR, MID-ASCENT - NIGHT

A tiny wooden cabin, varnished the color of old violins, climbing a steep rail through black pine forest. Brass lamps sway on their hooks. Rain hammers the glass. Below, the lights of a mountain town shrink to a handful of sparks.

A woman sits upright on the forward bench, her coat buttoned to the throat. ODALYS FERREIRA-MBEKI is forty, with a face carved from patience and a leather satchel clutched in both hands. She watches the man across from her, and she never blinks.

He is MARCELLUS HOLLINGWORTH-NAKAMURA, sixty, silver at the temples, gloves of fine kid. A cane lies across his lap. He smiles with his mouth only.

ODALYS
You're late.

MARCELLUS
The weather.

ODALYS
It's always the weather with you.

He taps the cane once against the floor. She flinches, barely. He notices.

MARCELLUS
You have it?

She does not answer. She lifts the satchel an inch, lets it fall back.

MARCELLUS (CONT'D)
Show me.

ODALYS
Not until the top.

MARCELLUS
We are not going to the top.

The car shudders. Something groans beneath the floor, steel on steel, and the lamps swing hard. Odalys grips the bench. He doesn't move at all.

ODALYS
What did you do?

MARCELLUS
Nothing yet.

The cabin stops. Dead in the middle of the slope. Rain on the glass. Wind in the cables.

He leans forward, resting both hands on the cane.

MARCELLUS (CONT'D)
Now. Show me.

Her hand slides inside the satchel. It comes out holding a small brass revolver, aimed at his chest. He looks at it, and for the first time, his smile changes.

MARCELLUS (CONT'D)
Ah. There she is.

ODALYS
Cut the cable, Marcellus.

MARCELLUS
That isn't how it works.

ODALYS
Cut it.

He lifts the cane. Twists the silver handle. A thin blade slides out, gleaming. He turns it in the lamplight, almost fondly.

MARCELLUS
It's a long way down, Odalys.

She cocks the hammer.
